Unreleased
---
Feature
- DockStyleColors::accentSoft, and the accent-soft token it fills: the wash a
  button hovers with - enough accent to read as emphasis, enough surface to stay
  a hover and not a fill. A theme spells it as a token of its own; the set has
  to derive it, since no palette role means "the accent, but faint", and it is
  mixed from the accent towards the window for the same reason the sheet asks
  rather than guesses.
- The dock's icon buttons state their own hover. A wash the sheet does not name
  is the platform's to choose, and every light theme was getting the platform's:
  a pale wash with rounded corners, which reads as a shape pasted on the bar
  rather than as the button lighting up. The buttons now take the accent-soft
  wash the ribbon's buttons take and square the corners - a hover is a wash with
  no box of its own, so there is nothing to round.
- The dock sheets take their colours from a DockStyleColors instead of reading
  palette() roles and literals inside the sheet. Every declaration that named a
  colour now carries a placeholder, and the new overload
  dockStyleSheet(const DockStyleColors &, bool) substitutes them; the existing
  dockStyleSheet(bool) stays and renders with DockStyleColors::fromPalette() of
  the application palette, which is what the sheet used to resolve to. An
  application that maps a theme's roles onto the set gets the dock painted in
  that theme; one that does not gets exactly what it had.
  The set separates two things the sheet had been conflating: where a colour
  sits in the dock (surface, divider, text, ...) and what goes on top of the
  accent (onAccent and its two washes). The second is why #ffffff was a literal:
  palette(highlightedtext) is not a name a style sheet's palette() lookup
  accepts, but QPalette::HighlightedText is reachable from C++, which is where
  fromPalette() asks for it. Two values flip direction on a dark window instead
  of being a straight role lookup: the divider (a separator has to move away
  from the surface) and the low contrast step under the side tab marks (a black
  wash on a dark surface is no wash at all).
  A field left invalid falls back per field, so a partial mapping still yields a
  complete sheet; an unresolved placeholder refuses the whole sheet and says so,
  rather than letting Qt drop the declaration silently.
  tst_DockWindow::styleSheetIsReadFromTheApplication
- The glyphs say which of two surfaces they are on rather than which colours to
  use: the ordinary one is painted in QPalette::WindowText and the emphasized
  one - a focused tab, everything on an auto hide title bar - in
  QPalette::HighlightedText, the pair of the accent fill. Both are asked in C++
  because a style sheet cannot ask for them: palette() accepts neither
  highlightedtext nor brighttext, and drops the declaration without a word.
  The dock had an overload that took a DockStyleColors instead, so that an
  application could hand the icons the same set it handed the sheet. It is gone
  again, unreleased: a glyph that takes a colour from anywhere but the palette is
  not the ribbon's model, and the set's on-accent was the last place where the
  sheet and the icons could disagree about the surface they were on. The palette
  overload is the one that shipped in 4.5.0 and the only one now.
  tst_DockIconProvider::colorsReachTheGlyph
- The focused treatment is selected by a dynamic property instead of by which
  fragment the sheet was assembled from, so it lives in base.css with the rest of
  the look. It had to be a fragment of its own because one of its rules is not
  about focus at all: every title bar reserves the two pixels the focused one
  paints in the accent, so that gaining the focus moves a colour and not the
  panel's content - and that reservation is for every panel, focused or not,
  which the focus state cannot express (a panel that has not been in focus yet
  has no "focused" property at all, and would be the one panel that jumps).
  DockTitleBar now sets "focusHighlighting" on itself when
  DockManager::FocusHighlighting is on, and the sheet asks for it; the flag is
  still what turns both that and the focus controller off, so an application that
  never sets it gets none of the treatment, exactly as before.
  What this buys is that the rules can travel with the look they belong to - a
  theme can carry them, which a fragment chosen in C++ cannot be. The
  focusHighlighting parameter of dockStyleSheet() no longer selects anything and
  both values return the same sheet; it stays because the signature is published,
  and its documentation says so.
  tst_DockWindow::styleSheetIsReadFromTheApplication,
  tst_DockTitleBar::reservesTheFocusRuleOnlyWhenTheFlagIsOn
- The dock's rules are one part of a theme now, in the same sheet as the
  ribbon's: the bundled themes are generated with them, spelled in the theme's
  own tokens (a separator is the border role, a raised surface the secondary
  one, the colour that reads on the accent the one the ribbon writes as
  text-on-fill), and an emphasized fill is the theme's deep accent fill with its
  pair of a label, the same fill a ribbon tab selected in the accent family
  wears. One source feeds both halves - res/dockstyle/base.css is the sheet an
  application with no theme renders from its palette, and the same file the
  generator lays into every theme - so a rule cannot exist in one and not the
  other, and the test that reads both sides compares their selectors.
  dockPlatformStyleSheet() hands out the piece a theme cannot have, the rules
  that belong to one desktop and only the built library knows; it is empty off
  X11, and appending it after a theme's sheet is the whole of the wiring.
  dockStyleSheet() keeps its published signatures and is the sheet an
  application installs when there is no theme. A theme that says nothing about
  the dock draws the dock the way a theme that says nothing about the ribbon
  draws the ribbon: unstyled.
  tst_QxThemeBundle::applicationSheetKeepsTheThemeOnTop,
  tst_QxThemeBundle::theThemeStatesTheDocksOwnRules

Bugfix
- A tab bar filled its own box with the window colour - the palette's answer,
  where every colour around it is the sheet's. In a light theme the two differ,
  so the run of bar between the last tab and the buttons read as a strip of a
  third colour, wider than the tabs it carried: a QTabBar fills by default and
  nothing told it not to, and a dark theme only hid it because there the global
  QWidget rule paints the same value. The bar and the two containers inside it
  step aside; a tab states its own fill and is named outright, so it still
  wins.
- A docked title bar's buttons carried a face of their own. A rule that names
  any box property hands the button to the stylesheet's painting, and one that
  states no background leaves the face to the base style - which paints a light
  button face, a patch sitting on the bar in every light theme and the same
  colour back in a dark one, where it only passed unnoticed. The buttons state
  no background now and step back in on hover and press (accent-soft and the
  pressed wash, the states the tab close button already takes); the close
  button's background:none meant the same thing and is spelled transparent,
  since a wash the sheet does not name falls back to the window colour.
- An auto hide container's title bar was painted over by the things on it. The
  bar is filled with the accent, and its own children tile it end to end - the
  label carrying the title, the spacer pushing the buttons to the far end, the
  buttons - none of which had a rule of its own, so each painted the window
  colour over the fill it sits on; measured on a soft-dark dock demo, 45% of a
  216px bar still showed the accent. The spacer was the widest of them and the
  one a sheet could not reach: it sets a style sheet on itself, a widget's own
  outranks the application's, and "background: none" sets no background, so the
  widget filled itself with the palette's window colour instead. Its sheet now
  says transparent, and the sheet's auto hide rules cover the bar's children
  without naming the private class a type selector cannot see.
- The buttons on an auto hide container's title bar kept the surface glyph
  colour. The buttons are built before anything knows the container will sit on
  the accent fill, and neither showing the window nor a later palette change
  re-applies icons to a container that has not been shown yet - so on a light
  scheme the glyph came out black on the accent, the exact case the tint exists
  for. Showing the container re-applies the icons beneath it now, which is the
  moment the fill it sits on becomes real. An application that never registered
  icons is untouched: refreshDockIcons() has nothing to hand out and changes
  nothing.
  tst_DockAutoHideContainer::glyphsFollowTheSurfaceOnShow

Cleanup
- The four dock style sheets became two fragments. They had been four complete
  copies of one look - default, focus_highlighting, and the _linux pair - because
  the look has two independent modifiers (FocusHighlighting and the X11 desktop)
  and the four combinations were written out separately. Four copies of the same
  366 to 485 lines drift, and these had: the splitter handle kept its fill only
  in the narrower of two selectors, the tabs-menu indicator and the tab icon size
  disagreed between variants, and the auto hide buttons' border was cleared on
  one platform only. The look is now res/dockstyle/base.css, appended by
  x11.css on an X11 build, and dockStyleSheet() joins them in that order. An
  application that listed the old file names in its own .qrc - rather than
  building in res/dockstyle.qrc - needs the two new ones.
  Two differences are deliberate: the close button keeps its 1px hover border
  under focus highlighting, and the tab icon size is 16px in every variant (it
  used to be set in the focus sheets only). Both are the same rule stated once
  instead of twice with a difference nobody meant.
  tst_DockWindow::styleSheetIsReadFromTheApplication

0.3.0
---
Feature
- DockWindow gained saveStateToFile() and restoreStateFromFile(), which write and
  read a layout as a plain XML file. saveState() has always built XML underneath,
  but the default configuration compresses it before returning, so a saved layout
  could only travel as an opaque QByteArray - right for QSettings, useless as a
  file a person can open, hand-edit or diff. The two new methods share saveState()'s
  writer through an internal helper and ask it not to compress; the reader goes
  through the same restoreState(), which already accepted uncompressed XML. An
  empty path, a file that will not open, and a version mismatch all return false
  without disturbing the current layout. tst_DockWindow::stateFile
- DockDrag carries a dock widget over a native drag, and DockWindow accepts one.
  Docking moved a dock widget with plain mouse events, which only works inside
  one process - the object dragged is the object dropped - so nothing could ever
  arrive from elsewhere. A drag now carries a payload naming the dock widget, and
  the window accepts a drop of it: a dock widget of its own that was closed comes
  back, and every drop is reported through the new dockWidgetDropped() signal and
  its position, because a dock widget that is not in this process can only be
  built by the application. The payload is UTF-8 text with a signature line, so a
  receiver that is not built on this library can still read it, and only that mime
  type is accepted - which is what keeps the in-process mouse dragging, that
  produces no QDrag, unaffected. tst_DockDrag
- An auto hide container can slide in and out, behind the new
  DockManager::AutoHideAnimationEnabled flag. Auto hiding has always been an
  instant show() and hide() - there was no animation anywhere in this library -
  and that stays the default, because a container that slides is a visual change
  and nothing verifies looks here. With the flag set, the container travels into
  place from the side bar it belongs to: the animation drives the widget's own
  geometry, so the container still positions itself and the flag only decides how
  it arrives, never where it belongs - which is also what the test holds on to
  rather than the animation itself. tst_DockAutoHideContainer::collapseSlidesIntoPlace
- The dock widgets carry accessible names and roles. Docking paints its own
  containers, so the default accessibility saw unnamed Clients: a dock panel, its
  tabs and the side bar had no name for an assistive tool to announce and no role
  to tell them apart from plain frames. DockWidget and DockTab now mirror their
  title into accessibleName (the '&' mnemonic stripped, kept in step with
  windowTitleChanged and setText), and a QAccessible factory, installed by the
  DockWindow constructor, reports the panel as Pane, the tabs as PageTab, and the
  tab bar and the side bar as PageTabList - the same treatment the ribbon gave its
  painted page and group. tst_DockAccessible

- DockWindow no longer styles itself. The four dock style sheets used to be
  compiled into the library and applied to the DockWindow on construction, which
  put the dock's appearance beyond the application's reach: a widget-level sheet
  outranks the application-level one, so an application that set a dark theme
  still received a DockWindow the library had already styled. The sheets moved to
  res/dockstyle.qrc, which the application builds in, and are handed out by the
  new qxdock::dockStyleSheet() - the application reads the text it wants and
  applies it through setStyleSheet(), the same move the ribbon themes made in
  B12. DockWindow carries and applies no stylesheet of its own.
  tst_DockWindow::carriesNoStyleSheetOfItsOwn
  tst_DockWindow::styleSheetIsReadFromTheApplication

- The built-in icons follow the application palette. The dock's SVG icons shipped
  with a hard-coded black, so on a dark system theme the buttons stayed black on
  a dark title bar - only the platform style icons, which the library fell back to
  when no custom icon was registered, were theme-aware. The four style sheets no
  longer set the icons through qproperty-icon, and the library offers
  qxdock::applyDockIcons(palette) and qxdock::refreshDockIcons(root) so an
  application can tint the SVGs to any palette and re-apply them when the palette
  changes - the same opt-in shape as dockStyleSheet(). An application that never
  calls them gets the platform style icons, which were already theme-aware; the
  docking library imposes no look of its own, it only hands out helpers.

Bugfix
- An auto hide overlay jumped into place instead of sliding. collapseView()
  opened the container, put it at its final geometry through updateSize(), then
  slid it in from the side bar it belongs to - but the slide drives the widget's
  own geometry, and every frame posted a QResizeEvent that the container's own
  qApp event filter caught. That filter's Resize branch never checked which
  widget had resized, so it called updateSize() on the container's own resize,
  and updateSize() stops any running slide; the first frame therefore killed the
  animation and the container snapped to its final position. The branch now
  reacts only to a resize of the dock container (the parent) - the case that
  means the window was resized and the overlay must be realigned - and ignores
  resizes of the overlay and its children, which are slide- or layout-driven and
  must not restart the layout computation. tst_DockAutoHideContainer::
  collapseSlidesIntoPlace still holds the end state; the interruption was a
  runtime feedback loop the geometry assertion could not see.
- The dock's own text and its state fills followed the dark scheme. The four
  style sheets had only ever been seen against one scheme - the demo carried no
  theme of its own until now - and four rules in them turned out to say
  something that is only true of a light one.
  An inactive tab's label was palette(dark): QPalette::Dark is derived from the
  window colour, so on a dark palette it is a near black and the label vanished
  into its own tab. It reads palette(placeholder) now, the role Qt gives to
  "this text, but quieter", which is dimmed from the scheme's own text colour.
  The text that sits on a highlight fill - the focused tab, the auto hide title,
  the title of an active floating window - was palette(light), which describes
  the surface rather than the fill: a dark grey label on the accent in a dark
  scheme, and in a light one the rule lost entirely, because [focused="true"]
  ties with the [activeTab="true"] rule above it on specificity and Qt keeps the
  earlier of two equally specific rules - so a light dock showed its selected
  tab in black on the accent. The selector now names both attributes, and the
  colour is #ffffff: the role that would say it (palette(highlightedtext),
  palette(brighttext) being the same value) is not among the names a style
  sheet's palette() lookup accepts, so the declaration would be dropped, and the
  accent fill is #0078d4 in both schemes anyway - which is how the ribbon themes
  write their text-on-fill.
  A label matched by a style sheet rule also has its box painted, and with no
  background to paint it the style sheet fell back on the style's own window
  colour - #1f1f1f for windows11 in dark, neither the tab's nor the panel's -
  so every tab wore a darker panel down its middle. The rule says transparent
  and the pixel goes back to the tab.
  The close button's hover and press fills were rgba(0, 0, 0, 16/24/48), a
  black wash: four levels of difference over a dark tab, which is no feedback at
  all. They read palette(light) and palette(mid) now - hover lifts the button
  off the surface, press settles it back in, opposite directions so the two
  states tell each other apart. palette(light) used to be the press fill, which
  was wrong the other way round: it steps towards the light like the hover fill
  does, and on a near-white window lighter() clamps it to #ffffff, the same
  colour the hover fill has there. On a focused tab the accent is the surface,
  so those two steps do not apply; there hover and press are white washes,
  deeper on press.
  The tinted icons turned white on hover. The focused variants of the SVGs are
  white copies of the normal shape, and applyDockIcons() mapped their white to
  BrightText - which is the highlight text, white in either scheme, so hover
  left the glyph white while the hover fill it now sits on is #ffffff in a light
  scheme: the button was there, the picture of closing was not. Both states are
  drawn over the window colour, so the white maps to the window text colour now
  and the glyph reads the same on hover as it does at rest, whatever the theme.
- An auto hide container's tab covered the title bar it sits in. That title bar
  is filled with palette(highlight) and the tab bar is laid out inside it, so
  the tab's own active fill - the window gradient, in the activeTab rule - drew
  a grey patch over the accent, and the label kept the colour it uses over the
  window colour. The four sheets now let the tab step aside inside an auto hide
  container: no fill, no border, and #ffffff for the label, matching the focused
  tab. The selector spells out both [activeTab] forms, because the activeTab
  rules outrank a bare type selector and a comma list counts as its least
  specific member. Hover and press on that close button are white washes, the
  same relationship the focused tab has, since the accent is the surface there.
- The buttons that sit on the highlight fill wore the window text glyph. The
  focused tab's close button and every button of an auto hide title bar paint
  over palette(highlight), where the window text tint all but disappears into
  the accent - a black cross on #0078d4. The icon provider now hands out a
  second variant built for that surface, in which every state is the highlight
  text colour (the disabled one a translucent white, the only step available on
  a saturated fill), and refreshDockIcons() picks it for a button whose parent
  tab is focused or whose ancestor is an auto hide container. DockTab switches
  its close button between the two when the focus controller sets the "focused"
  property, so the glyph follows the fill the tab is painting on. An application
  that never calls applyDockIcons() gets no variant and keeps the platform icons
  it already had.

0.2.3
---
Bugfix
- DockPanel::minimumSizeHint() asked whether the title bar is visible when it
  meant whether the panel has one, and so left the title bar height out of the
  bound while the window had not been shown yet - isVisible() is false for the
  whole tree until then. Everything else in this library asks that question of
  isHidden() (openedDockPanels(), topLevelDockPanel(), the drop overlay), so
  this was the one place that disagreed with its neighbours. The hint is asked
  again rather than cached, so the value corrects itself once the window is
  shown; what made it worth fixing is that the contradiction was invisible,
  not that the damage lasted. tst_DockPanel::minimumSizeHintKeepsTheTitleBar
- DockWindow::loadStylesheet() dropped the result of opening the compiled-in
  stylesheet and, if the open failed, went on to set the empty string it had
  read - an empty stylesheet being how an application's own stylesheet gets
  wiped. The resource cannot fail to open in a correct build, so this fixes the
  shape of the code rather than a symptom anybody reported: it now leaves the
  stylesheet alone and says why. tst_DockWindow::builtInStylesheetIsApplied

Cleanup
- Drop the Qt 5.12.2 branch in DockFloatingContainer::event(). The threshold
  sits below the 5.15 minimum K1 set, so the "#if" side is the one that always
  ran: the workaround for the wrong mouse button that
  QEvent::NonClientAreaMouseButtonPress reports from 5.12.2 on is now
  unconditional, and the "#else" side that checked for the left mouse button is
  gone.

Other
- DockWindow no longer calls QApplication::setActiveWindow(), deprecated as of
  Qt 6.12, when a restore from minimized should make the window active again; it
  calls QWidget::activateWindow(), the documented replacement, which asks for
  the same thing. Measured before the swap, under the offscreen platform the
  window is already active once it is shown, so no observable state tells the
  two calls apart - which is why the swap has no test of its own.
- The X11 helpers no longer include a QPA header. x11_connection() reached the
  X connection through QPlatformNativeInterface and its "connection" key, which
  is Qt's private platform API and needs <qpa/qplatformnativeinterface.h> - a
  header Qt 6.12 does not install, so this library stopped compiling on it. Qt 6
  now asks for QNativeInterface::QX11Application, the documented replacement:
  typed instead of string keyed, and public, so it needs no private header. Qt 5
  keeps the old call, having no QNativeInterface. The connection is the same
  either way; only the way of asking for it changed.

0.2.2
---
Bugfix
- DockWindow left the features it has locked globally uninitialised. Nothing set
  DockWindowPrivate::m_lockedDockWidgetFeatures, and DockWidget::features()
  answers with the widget's own features minus that value, so every dock widget
  that belonged to a window reported a feature set with arbitrary bits removed
  until the application happened to call lockDockWidgetFeaturesGlobally() - whose
  first call could in turn be swallowed by its "already in that state" early
  return. The field now starts at NoDockWidgetFeatures, which is what the
  accessor documents for a window that has never locked anything.
- DockWidget crashed when a widget with DeleteContentOnClose was closed without
  content. hideDockWidget() deleted the content unconditionally, while the class
  elsewhere runs with no content on purpose: takeWidget() is the documented way
  for a caller to take it back, and showDockWidget() rebuilds it from the widget
  factory. Closing such a widget dereferenced a null pointer.
- Pinning an already pinned widget crashed. DockWidget::setAutoHide() and
  DockPanel::setAutoHide() both take DockSideBarNone to mean "no particular
  side was asked for", but only substituted a real side on the path that creates
  the auto hide container. The path that moves an already pinned widget to
  another side bar passed None on, and a container only ever builds a side bar
  for the four sides, so the lookup came back null and was dereferenced. The
  "Pin" entry of the tab context menu reaches it: it calls setAutoHide(true)
  with the default area, and it is offered for a widget that is already pinned.

Cleanup
- Remove three private fields that were maintained but never read: the container's
  cached visible panel count (visibleDockPanelCount() counts on demand), the flag
  recording whether the pending auto hide timer meant show or hide (the timer
  simulates a press on the tab either way, so the answer never reached anything),
  and the floating container's drag start position (dragging is driven by the
  mouse position captured when the drag starts).
- The title bar's context menu asked whether the panel is auto hidden inside the
  branch where that is already known to be false, so one of the two labels it
  offered could never be shown. The menu still reads the same; the dead arm is gone.
- DockAutoHideContainer kept its own copy of "which side bar areas are
  horizontal" next to the one in DockUtils. The two differed only in their
  default, which is unreachable because a container is only ever built for one
  of the four sides - but that was enough for
  DockAutoHideContainerPrivate::isHorizontal() and
  DockAutoHideContainer::orientation() to be free to contradict each other about
  the same area. The copy is gone and both consult DockUtils now.
- DockSideTab::mouseMoveEvent() tested for DockDraggingTab with the code it was
  supposed to run commented out, and the state is not reachable for a side tab
  in the first place: startFloating() is the only writer of that drag state and
  it only ever stores FloatingWidget. A side tab cannot be reordered, so the
  branch is gone.

0.2.1
---
Cleanup
- Remove the Qt version branches below 5.15, which K1 set as the minimum: the
  hand-written qByteArrayToHex/toHexLower helpers that existed for Qt below 5.9, the
  QPixmap::devicePixelRatio fallback, the QString::SkipEmptyParts fallback, the
  QFlags::setFlag fallback and the QFontMetrics::width fallbacks in DockLabel

Bugfix
- DockPanel: do not reopen an auto hide panel while a widget is inserted into
  it, which unpinned the widget again and left an empty panel in the root
  splitter
- QX_DOCK_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_DOCK_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_DOCK_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version being
  built - (0,2,0) > (0,2,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to false on
  every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays declared for
  as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the cutoff is what
  compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries

Test
- cover DockGroup, DockGroupManager and DockSubWindow with tst_DockGroup
  (structural API, group-name validation, and perspective save/load round-trip)

0.2.0
---
- Annotate and perfect components
- add perspective and group
- fix: restoreState causes programe crash
- fix build failure on linux, qt6, qt5.11.1

